description:
Would you be interested in adding local/with functions for file locking? I've written some prototypes that seem to be working:

```r
library(filelock)
local_lock <- function(path, ..., suffix = ".lock", .local_envir = parent.frame()) {
    lock_file_path <- str_c(path, suffix)
    local_lck <- filelock::lock(lock_file_path)
    defer(unlock(local_lck), envir = .local_envir)
}

with_lock <- function(path, code, ...) {
    local_lock(path = path, ...)
    code
}
```

Testing:

```r
library(callr)

# This gets the lock (and then releases it when the process ends)
r(function() { lck <- filelock::lock("TEMP.lock", timeout = 0); print(lck); filelock::unlock(lck); invisible(NULL); }, stdout = "", stderr = "" )

# this time the same r() call doesn't get the lock and prints NULL, because with_file_lock already holds the lock in the main R process.
with_lock("TEMP", {
    r(function() { lck <- filelock::lock("TEMP.lock", timeout = 0); print(lck); invisible(NULL); }, stdout = "", stderr = "" )
})

# This time we do get the lock, because we're running in the same process.
with_lock("TEMP", {
    lck <- filelock::lock("TEMP.lock", timeout = 0); 
    print(lck)
    rm(lck)
})
```

This function can be quite useful for people developing packages meant to be ran in an R Markdown/Quarto document, or simply for users who would like to execute an operation when knitr exits.

I actually have a package ([pakret](https://github.com/arnaudgallou/pakret)) that would greatly benefit from it. My package cites and references R packages on the fly in R Markdown/Quarto. pakret stores bibtex entries in an environment and populates a `.bib` file when knitr exits using `withr::defer()`:

``` r
.onLoad <- function(...) {
  do.call(set, .__settings__)
  if (is_rendering_context()) {
    set(render = TRUE)
    bib_init()
    terminate()
  }
}

terminate <- function() {
  withr::defer(bib_write(), envir = parent.frame(5L))
}
```

Since `defer()` is called inside a code chunk (when loading pakret), it won't automatically execute when knitr exits due to the following condition that is too strict:

https://github.com/r-lib/withr/blob/c8539fe735a5b6cdb496c68bca8248f8b6b4d419/R/defer.R#L96-L96C71

For simplicity, I used a fixed number to retrieve the running frame of knitr, which happens to be quite fragile. For example, using `conflicted::conflicts_prefer()` modifies the frame stack and breaks my package at the same time (see arnaudgallou/pakret#30 for a reprex).

